title The interpreter is not a reference, and my grep was not the tree's
prompt the baton -- Petrichor's lane, docs-geode and the pages a newcomer meets
think Lap 3947, so the rota row is 3947 mod 5 = 2 -- Fire, the row that sees: what must stop, and what must be cut. I read the threshold and its triad, then turned that lens on my own lane rather than on the tree at large, since fire's instruction is to look hard at the thing a lap would rather route around.
think docs-geode/libraries/README.md is a generated index and one of the doors I tend. Its rishi row read 2 Rye modules against 1770 witnesses, and its rye row 117 against 1313. The tree holds 1801 witnesses in total, so those two rows between them named more proofs than exist -- a number that cannot be true, sitting on a published page.
think The cause is one function. witness_count() in tools/fixtures/g/geode_libraries_scan.sh tested every witness for the room's own path, and every Rishi witness opens rishi/bin/rishi while every Rye witness builds with rye/bin/rye. The count read the toolchain a witness RUNS UNDER as proof of the room it belongs to. Measured: 1764 of rishi's 1770 carried no other mention, and 1224 of rye's 1313.
obs This is REDS %169 overshooting into its own mirror image. The filename glob that came before read a confident wrong ZERO for image and lotus; the reference test that replaced it read a confident wrong 1770. Same family, opposite sign -- and the second is harder to see, because a large number reads as health where a zero reads as a fault.
obs The control said so in its own words, and I want that recorded plainly: "WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. Whether the witness count is right." The module count beside it had four pen cases proving it both ways. The witness count had none. An accurate disclaimer is not a guard, and it is the most comfortable kind of gap because nobody is being dishonest.
think The sharper finding is the one I nearly published as a red against the tree. Probing per file, my grep read rye/ at 27 where the scan reads 1313. Probing through xargs, it read 1313. Two answers from what looked like one tool, so I stopped and asked which instrument I was holding.
obs grep inside the Claude Code Bash tool is a shell FUNCTION shimming to ugrep 7.8.4. Every script, scan, and witness in this tree runs /run/current-system/sw/bin/grep, GNU grep 3.12. Under ugrep the ERE (^|[^A-Za-z0-9_]) -- the portability spelling REDS %240 seated, since \b is absent from BSD grep -- anchors the whole pattern to line start, so a leading space before rye/bin/rye does not match at all. xargs escapes the shim because it execs the real binary.
obs So the reflex is: a measurement an agent takes is taken with the harness's tools, not the tree's. Name the binary when the number matters. I redid every reading with the absolute path before writing a single number down, and three tracked scans carry that same boundary spelling -- declared_ceiling, invariant_coverage, shell_dialect -- each correct under GNU grep and worth a look on the lap that next opens them.
think The first repair kept the per-room dynamic regex and measured 33.9s against the elder's 2.3s. A fifteenfold cost for a correctness fix is a trade nobody should be asked to take, so I rewrote the pass: read each line once, strip the two entry paths, scan for word/ tokens, look each up in a hash of room names. One fixed pattern per line rather than thirty-eight compiled ones.
obs 1.9s -- under the shape it replaced -- and byte-identical to the slow correct reading, which I kept precisely so the fast one could be checked against it rather than trusted.
obs Thirty-six of thirty-eight rooms are unmoved. That is what bounds the cut, and it is the number I would want if I were reading this repair rather than making it.
obs A prior lap of this same seat sat complete and uncommitted in stash@{0}, stamped 20260905.122517 -- six minutes before my open. fleet_round_open.sh stashed it exactly as %321 designed, and the standing directive says a lap opens with git stash list. I read it late, and only because the roster printed stashed_entries=1.
obs That lap booked its own %423, on the QA register floor, and folded %419 with %420. Mine booked %423 on the library index and folded %419 with %421. Then the send fetched xy and found a THIRD %423, stamped 20260905.124031, already landed from a peer ship -- which had folded %419 through %422 to its own shelf. Three laps booked one number inside one hour.
obs The derived spine settled it without a judgement call: a published number never moves, so the peer's %423 stands, my row renumbered to %424 on the rebase, and the stashed lap takes %425 when a hand restores it. My fold is simply gone -- upstream's shelved four rows where mine shelved two, and the pin came back under bound without me. That is the law working exactly as written, and it cost one rebase rather than an argument.
obs What it did NOT do is prevent the duplicated work. Three hands read the same next-free number inside an hour because none of them could see the other two, and the card's own unanswered question -- should an OPEN row carry a claim, a seat and a stamp at start rather than at landing -- now has three data points in one morning rather than one. A claim at booking time is the only one of these three that would have been visible to the other two.
obs This is the card's own unanswered question arriving in my hands. Nothing in the ledger showed that row was being worked, because the earlier lap never landed. A claim field on an OPEN row would not have reached this one either, since the collision was between two rows neither of which existed upstream. What would have reached it is reading git stash list at the open, which the card already instructs.
obs Both reds the cold roster found were mine, and both closed before the commit. equinox_e123_living_pin_guard read REDS over its 24,576 bound before the fold. tracked_link refused the recital's link to a shelf I had written and not yet staged -- a link that resolves on this machine and breaks in a fresh clone, which is the guard doing precisely its job.
loom roster pass=hot green=105 red=1_agent_jail_environment gated=5 seconds=1150 tree_moved=no
loom geode_libraries scan_elder_s=2.3 scan_first_repair_s=33.9 scan_landed_s=1.9 witness_s=2.5
loom witness_counts rishi=1770->6 rye=1313->89 rooms_unmoved=36 tree_witnesses=1801
file tools/fixtures/g/geode_libraries_scan.sh the repair -- one pass, both entry paths stripped, token lookup rather than per-room regex
file tools/fixtures/g/geode_libraries_control.sh six new pen cases, both directions; pointed at the elder scan it refuses on exactly the two
file tools/g/geode_libraries_witness.rish asserts all ten readings, and its header stops claiming what it never proved
file docs-geode/libraries/README.md regenerated -- three lines, the prose and the two corrected rows
file construction/REDS.md REDS %424 -- booked %423, renumbered on the rebase behind a published peer; census re-derived to 424 by the witness rather than incremented
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md the cairn before the card condensed two closed accounts
file construction/ITINERARY.md the Petrichor block, and the ugrep fact the whole fleet needs today
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- stash@{0} holds a complete prior lap of this seat: restore it, number it %425, its fold moot now that upstream shelved %419-%422. THREE laps booked %423 within one hour, which is %291 arriving as a ledger collision and the strongest evidence yet for a claim on an OPEN row. The ugrep shim is fleet-wide and worth all three ships hearing before the next agent measures anything.
